  - m_Id: 6593451503116294
    m_Localized: Danger Zone
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116295
    m_Localized: Trade Hub
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116296
    m_Localized: Port Access
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116297
    m_Localized: 'On the northeastern edge of the Great Wall lies Youzhou - a frontier
      hub from which the Tang launches troops against Khitan raids and campaigns
      toward Koguryo and Bohai. The city itself is modest: earthen ramparts, timber
      barracks, a noisy horse-and-felt market where Turkic and Khitan horsemen haggle
      with imperial buyers. Youzhou''s importance lies in its roads: the track from
      Liaodong meets here the mountain trail through the Yan hills and the post road
      south to Luoyang. The rebellion of An Lushan will ignite from this very region
      - the general gathers in these parts the triple governorate of Fanyang, Pinglu,
      and Hedong, building a power that will shake the whole empire.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116298
    m_Localized: 'The Tang capital spreads across eighty square li - a precise grid
      of boulevards, markets, and wards housing more than a million souls. The Western
      Market roars with Sogdian, Persian, Indian, and Arab voices: silk and porcelain
      change hands here alongside Tocharian horses and overseas spices sold at the
      roadside. In the Hufan wards foreigners build Nestorian churches and Manichaean
      chapels beside Daoist and Buddhist monasteries - the Tang court tolerates foreign
      faiths. The Imperial Secretariat and the Bureau of Six Departments govern a
      vast realm stretching from Korea to the Pamirs; the Hanlin Academy trains officials
      who have passed the rigorous keju examinations. From here the road west begins:
      beyond the Jade Gate the Silk Road opens.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116299
    m_Localized: 'Lanzhou stands where the Yellow River cuts through the loess plateaus
      in a sharp bend: the ferry crossings are expensive but unavoidable - this is
      the point where eastern China connects to the Hexi Corridor. Floating bridges
      of inflated ox-skin bags - the bayan - keep the crossing open even in flood
      season, though passage demands skill and light loads. The local market sells
      horses from the Tibetan plateau, wool from the Qiang highlanders, and salt
      from the Qaidam salt lakes. The city''s strategic importance is such that the
      Ministry of War maintains here a permanent supply headquarters for the western
      armies, and every merchant must register at the prefectural office before continuing
      the journey.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116300
    m_Localized: On the bank of the Huangpu river where it enters the Yangtze estuary
      lies a small fishing village called Hudu - the future Shanghai. The Yangtze
      - the main trading artery of southern China - carries past it barges loaded
      with silk from Hangzhou and rice from Sichuan; river boats stop here to take
      on water and salt from coastal evaporation pans. Fresh fish and reed matting
      are the chief local goods; sea salt produced on the muddy shores travels to
      inland markets. While Guangzhou and Quanzhou receive overseas merchants the
      Yangtze mouth remains an internal river hub for transferring southern cargoes
      to the northern route along the Grand Canal.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116301
    m_Localized: 'On the southern shore of the picturesque Xihu - West Lake - Hangzhou
      spreads among tea plantations and sericulture estates that yield more revenue
      to the treasury than some entire provinces. The local mian silk is prized for
      its particular softness: thread is reeled directly at the cocoon pools before
      the silk can dry. The Great Southern Canal - part of the Sui Grand Canal -
      links Hangzhou to Luoyang and Chang''an: grain barges and silk bales travel
      north without reloading. The city''s weavers belong to guilds answerable to
      the Ministry of the Imperial Household; officials accept silk as tax payment
      at a fixed rate against silver. Green tea from the Tianmu mountains is already
      reaching the tables of connoisseurs in Chang''an.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116302
    m_Localized: 'Quanzhou - the Zaytun of Arab pilots - stands at the mouth of the
      Taiwan Strait on a bay with convenient anchorage. Under the Tang it is already
      a major maritime port: the Office of Maritime Trade collects dues here from
      Arab and Malay ships bringing pepper and camphor. Local shipyards build ocean-going
      junks with watertight bulkheads - a secret unknown to western shipbuilders.
      A Manichaean oratory and a Nestorian chapel stand beside Buddhist monasteries
      in the foreign merchants'' wards. Quanzhou porcelain - bluish-white with dense
      clay body - is beginning to build its reputation in the markets of Southeast
      Asia, though the Chang''an court still prefers kilns of the north.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116303
    m_Localized: 'Guangzhou breathes salt air, spices, and ship-timber resin: in
      the mouth of the Pearl River dozens of Arab, Persian, and Malay vessels lie
      at anchor awaiting the trade winds for the return voyage. The Office of Maritime
      Trade - the Shibosi - collects duties here and issues permits to foreign captains;
      Arab and Persian merchants live in a special ward called Dianliu under the
      supervision of an imperial official. From Guangzhou silk, porcelain, lacquerware,
      and tea travel westward; returning ships bring incense, pearls, tortoiseshell,
      and rare tropical timbers. The poet Zhang Jiuling, a native of this region
      and chief minister under Emperor Xuanzong, enhanced the city''s standing by
      reforming its trade regulations.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6593451503116304
    m_Localized: Caravan speed +20%
