  - m_Id: 7217665172037673
    m_Localized: Trabzon
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037674
    m_Localized: Constantinople
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037675
    m_Localized: 'Among the bare rocks of the Tian Shan, Aksu spreads as a green
      patch of orchards and fields watered by glacial rivers flowing down from the
      heights. Here the northern route around the Taklamakan makes its first long
      halt after barren steppes: merchants mend damaged harness, swap exhausted camels
      for fresh ones, and stock grain for the grueling crossing to Kashgar. Aksu
      answers to the Tang military protectorate of Anxi and serves as a rear base
      for the garrisons guarding the passes against Turkic raids. Sogdian traders
      keep permanent warehouses here, while local Uyghur merchants supply horses
      and felt to imperial supply trains.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037676
    m_Localized: 'The scent of myrrh and tanned leather hangs over the covered lanes
      of Aleppo - market streets that have never stood empty since Assyrian times.
      Under Abbasid rule the city became a transit hub between Mesopotamia and the
      Syrian coast: bales of silk arriving from Persia are reloaded here alongside
      bolts of purple linen dispatched eastward. Aleppo soap, boiled from olive oil
      and laurel extract, is bought by merchants from across the Levant. The city
      is graced by the Umayyad mosque and a citadel atop an ancient tell, from which
      sentries watch the roads toward Antioch and Raqqa.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037677
    m_Localized: Where the grey-green Ganges swallows the yellow Yamuna stands Prayaga
      - the sacred confluence that Brahmin priests call the very spot where the gods
      stirred the nectar of immortality. Thousands of pilgrims camp on the sandbars
      at great festival times, bathing in the three rivers - the visible Ganges and
      Yamuna and the invisible Saraswati said to flow underground. Trading boats
      carrying grain, sugar, and cotton ply the Ganges here, linking Bengal to the
      north. The site falls under the protection of a raja of the Pratihara line,
      who patronises both Hindu Brahmin scholars and the Buddhist monasteries of
      the Pala realm.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037678
    m_Localized: 'Antioch sits in the valley of the Orontes where the Amanus mountains
      open toward the Syrian shore: basalt walls raised in Justinian''s day now carry
      Arab sentries. After the conquest of 637 it passed to the Umayyad Caliphate,
      and under the Abbasids it became a frontier post against Byzantium - both powers
      exchange embassies and prisoners through these gates. Silk, olive oil, and
      Antiochene glassware travel by merchant ship to Constantinople and Alexandria.
      A Nestorian community and a small Greek congregation live alongside the Muslim
      majority under the protection of dhimmi agreements - the poll tax opens doors,
      though not always hearts.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037679
    m_Localized: Anxi is the headquarters of the Great Anxi Protectorate, whose authority
      stretches from the eastern Tarim Basin to Sogdia. Within its rammed-earth walls
      the Tang stores imperial grain reserves and an arsenal, and on the parade ground
      officials of the Ministry of Personnel distribute orders to the garrisons of
      the four great oases. Merchants heading west must present their paizi travel
      pass here and pay duty into the imperial treasury. The desert horizon beyond
      the north gate holds the threat of Turgesh and Uyghur ambushes, while the southern
      road leads through Dunhuang toward the flanks of the Kunlun range. Here Chinese
      speech fades and a mixing of tongues begins - Sogdian, Tocharian, Uyghur -
      the first syllables of the western world.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037680
    m_Localized: 'Under the searing Nubian sun Aydhab looks like a scatter of mud-brick
      huts and warehouses, yet each year with the arrival of pilgrimage season the
      quay becomes a cauldron of people, camels, and rigging. Ships from Fustat and
      Aden anchor in the shallow bay, ferrying pilgrims to the Arabian shore at Jeddah,
      within easy reach of Mecca. Transit trade settles here too: Ethiopian captives,
      ivory, and ostrich feathers move northward, while Yemeni spices and Indian
      cloth head west into Egypt. The local Beja nomads hold a monopoly on camel
      transport between the Red Sea and the Nile valley.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037681
    m_Localized: "Madinat al-Salam - the City of Peace - was built by Caliph al-Mansur
      in just a few years as a perfect circle: four gates face the four cardinal
      points and at the center rises the golden-domed palace. The House of Wisdom
      translates Greek, Syriac, and Persian works on mathematics, astronomy, and
      medicine, drawing scholars from across the known world. Along the Nahraw\u0101n
      and Sar\u0101t canals ships arrive carrying rice from Khuzestan, spices from
      India, and silk from China; the Karkh market on the western bank of the Tigris
      hums with trade in a dozen languages. Abbasid caliphs receive here embassies
      from the Tang court and Tibetan kings, exchanging gifts - glass and horses
      for silk and paper."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037682
    m_Localized: 'Balkh is called the Mother of Cities: even the ruins of its Bactrian
      walls stand twice the height of present buildings, and every layer of soil
      holds sherds from Greek, Kushan, and Sasanian times. After the Arab conquest
      a Caliphate garrison is stationed here, yet the Persian Dari language and Zoroastrian
      families have held on in the suburbs. From the Badakhshan mines nearby comes
      lapis lazuli - the blue stone that colors frescoes from Samarkand to Baghdad;
      the cotton fields around the city supply cloth for all of Central Asia. Sogdian
      merchants keep transit warehouses in Balkh between Merv and Kabul, while local
      scholars copy Avestan texts in secrecy from zealous officials.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037683
    m_Localized: 'Bukhara lies in the fertile Zerafshan valley, surrounded by orchards
      of apricot and mulberry: sericulture here is so developed that local raw silk
      travels to Chang''an and Constantinople under its own name. After the Arab
      conquest the city is governed by the Persian Barmakid family - descended from
      local Buddhist priests who converted to Islam and rose to become viziers of
      the caliph. On the main bazaar Sogdian merchants, Arab tax collectors, and
      Chinese envoys haggle over musk, silver vessels, and cotton cloth. Bukharan
      craftsmen weave brocade shot with gold thread, purchased at courts from Baghdad
      to Chang''an.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037684
    m_Localized: 'On the eastern bank of the Nile beside the ancient Babylon fortress,
      an Arab army pitched its first camp in 641 and named it Fustat - the Tent.
      From a temporary bivouac it grew into the capital of Arab Egypt: whitewashed
      houses stretch along Trajan''s canal linking the Nile to the Red Sea, and the
      quay receives grain barges that feed Arabia. The mosque of Amr ibn al-As -
      the first on the African shore - stands at the city''s center, where Coptic
      priests and Muslim judges conduct their affairs side by side under the same
      authority. Egypt''s linen cloth, papyrus, and alum from the eastern desert
      travel to Baghdad and Mediterranean ports. The pyramids of Giza rise on the
      horizon - silent witnesses to the succession of rulers.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665172037685
    m_Localized: 'At the mouth of the Sabarmati river Cambay receives ships from
      across the Indian Ocean: Arab dhows anchor alongside Persian and Malay vessels,
      drawn by the wealth of Gujarat. Local weavers produce fine cotton called qutn
      in Arabic sources and bright indigo-dyed cloth that travels to Oman and on
      to Zanzibar. The port is controlled by trading clans of Gujarati Vaishya merchants;
      Arab factors maintain permanent offices here, trading dates, frankincense,
      and silver dirhams in exchange for cotton, teak, and black pepper. The tides
      in the Gulf of Cambay are treacherous - navigators know that entering the harbor
      must be timed precisely to the tidal schedule or a vessel will run aground.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231936
    m_Localized: 'Above the Golden Horn floats the dome of Hagia Sophia - the largest
      in the world, a reminder of Justinian''s power, though the present basileis
      fight on two fronts: Arabs press from east and south while Bulgars push from
      the north. The Theodosian walls have survived several Arab sieges, and the
      citizens give thanks for the fleet''s secret fire - Greek fire that burns enemy
      ships in the Bosphorus. The bazaars of the Mese trade in silk, contested and
      coveted: after Justinian spirited silkworm eggs out of China, Constantinople
      began producing its own pavoloke silk, yet Tang raw silk is still prized higher.
      Iconoclast disputes split the court and the Church, but merchants are more
      occupied with the customs duties at the Bosphorus than with theology.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231937
    m_Localized: 'Damascus smells of roses and iron smoke: the rose oil pressed here
      from May to July travels to Persia and India, while smiths in the steelworkers''
      quarter forge blades with a wavy pattern that resists both rust and dulling.
      Under the Umayyads Damascus was the Caliphate''s capital - the Umayyad court
      stood here and the treasury was kept here, but after the capital moved to Baghdad
      in 762 the city lost its political luster while keeping its commercial one.
      The Umayyad mosque, raised over the basilica of John the Baptist, astonishes
      with mosaic landscapes - green gardens said to depict the paradise of Allah.
      Damascene silk woven in geometric interlace travels north to Constantinople
      and east across the Palmyrene desert.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231938
    m_Localized: On the bank of the Yamuna lie the ruins of ancient Indraprastha
      - the city of the Pandavas from the Mahabharata - and beside them a modest
      settlement called Dilli, which local Rajput chieftains give over to a market
      and a river crossing. The Pratiharas rule northern Hindustan from Kannauj,
      and Dilli remains a humble market town on the road between the capital and
      the mountain passes of Aryavarta. Here horses and oxen are changed, iron from
      the Haryana mines and cotton from the Ghaggar valley change hands. Merchants
      traveling from Kabul into the heart of India make their first long halt here
      and pay toll to the Rajput collector.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231939
    m_Localized: The Mogao Caves hold thousands of Buddhist frescoes. Pilgrims and
      merchants pray here before the perilous crossing.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231940
    m_Localized: 'Gaza stands at the border of the green Syrian hills and the yellow
      Sinai wedge: in its harbor warehouses Egyptian linen, Palestinian olive oil,
      and Arabian frankincense are all heaped together. Under the Umayyads and then
      the Abbasids the city serves as a mustering point for Cairo and Damascus merchant
      caravans: camel trains for the crossing of Sinai to Fustat are assembled here.
      Gazan wines, famous across the Mediterranean since late-Roman times, are still
      produced by local Christian communities, though Muslim officials turn a blind
      eye for the sake of customs revenue. The tomb of Hashim - the Prophet''s grandfather
      - gives the city a special sanctity in the eyes of Muslim pilgrims traveling
      from Egypt to Mecca.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231941
    m_Localized: 'Hormuz lies on the mainland shore at the mouth of the Persian Gulf,
      within a day''s sail of the Arabian coast with a favorable wind. Through this
      port flows the trade of two seas: from the Indian Ocean come pepper and cinnamon
      from Malabar, pearls from Ceylon, and teak from the Malabar coast; from the
      Persian Gulf come dates, horses, and silver vessels moving inland. Persian
      navigators know the channel by heart and charge handsomely for piloting foreign
      ships. The Abbasid governor collects duties in kind - a share of cargo from
      every hull - and ships them to Basra for transfer onto river boats bound for
      Baghdad.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231942
    m_Localized: 'Jiuquan - the Wine Spring - takes its name from a legend: the general
      Huo Qubing ordered a jar of wine poured into the local spring to share it with
      his soldiers after a victory over the Xiongnu. Today a large Tang garrison
      stationed here guards the Hexi Corridor between the Qilian mountains and the
      Gobi sands. Along this narrow passage twenty to a hundred li wide runs the
      main thread of the Silk Road from Luoyang to Hami: every merchant convoy passes
      Jiuquan. Local armorers supply bows and spears to the forces of the Anxi Protectorate;
      the imperial granaries accept grain brought from beyond the Yellow River and
      dispatch it to the western garrisons.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231943
    m_Localized: 'Kabul sits in a valley at twelve hundred metres elevation, squeezed
      between spurs of the Hindu Kush: from here one pass leads north to Balkh and
      Sogdia, another east to Peshawar and the Indian plain. The Shahis of Kabulistan
      - a local Buddhist dynasty of Turkic origin - held their independence between
      the Tang, the Caliphate, and the Tibetans, trading frankincense, lapis lazuli,
      and sure-footed mountain horses. Arab armies approached the city several times,
      but the mountain trails and the fierce Kabuli archers drove the conquerors
      back. Languages here are thoroughly mixed: the Turkic nobility speaks its own
      dialect, merchants speak Dari, Buddhist monks speak Prakrit and Tocharian.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231944
    m_Localized: The great crossroads - here the northern and southern routes around
      the Taklamakan converge. The road west lies open.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231945
    m_Localized: 'From the glaciers of the Kunlun two rivers descend - the Yurunkash
      and the Karakash, white and black - and on their banks mountain folk gather
      jade: smooth pebbles the color of spinach and milk that travel to Chang''an
      at the price of silver. Khotan on the southern route around the Taklamakan
      has guarded the secret of sericulture for several centuries - legend holds
      that a Khotanese king learned the art from a Chinese princess who hid mulberry
      cocoons in her headdress. Local Buddhist monasteries flourish under the patronage
      of the Khotanese kings who maintain independence between Tang overlords and
      Tibetan viceroys; monks copy sutras in the Khotanese Saka language. Cloth bearing
      the Khotanese pattern - geometric medallions - is imitated by craftsmen from
      Sogdia to Iran.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231946
    m_Localized: 'Kucha is the most populous oasis on the northern route: within
      its rammed-earth walls live the Tocharian Kucheans, whose music and dances
      won such favor in Chang''an that Kuchean harpists and acrobats became indispensable
      guests at imperial banquets. The cave monasteries in the Kizil gorge are painted
      with frescoes depicting jatakas about the Buddha''s past lives - pigments are
      made here from local vitriol and imported lapis lazuli. The celebrated translator
      of Buddhist texts Kumarajiva was born in Kucha and from here was taken to China,
      where he rendered hundreds of sutras into Chinese. Kuchean iron and leather
      trade against Chinese silk and paper; along the road stand caravanserais whose
      innkeepers converse in three languages at once.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231947
    m_Localized: 'Lhasa lies in the wide valley of the Kyichu river at an elevation
      of thirty-five hundred metres: the air is so thin that newly arrived lowland
      merchants lose consciousness on the first climb. The red hill of Marpori is
      crowned by the Potala - the palace-monastery of King Songtsen Gampo who unified
      Tibet and embraced Buddhism from Indian and Chinese teachers. The Tibetan realm
      is now at the peak of its power: the tsenpo controls the trade routes between
      India and China, levying duties in musk and gold dust. The Jokhang monastery
      holds the Jowo statue - a golden Buddha gifted by Tang princess Wencheng. Tibetan
      warriors in lamellar armor with yak-tail plumes on their helmets are the terror
      of western China.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231948
    m_Localized: 'In the deep gorge where the Jinsha river makes its sharpest turn
      on the way to the sea the Naxi people built Lijiang across three hills joined
      by wooden bridges over crystal streams. Here the southwestern branch of the
      Silk Road crosses the Tea-Horse Road: compressed puerh tea in bamboo wrapping
      travels on mules over the passes into Tibet while sturdy Tibetan horses are
      driven back - horses the Tang need for cavalry. The dongba shamans of the Naxi
      preserve a script of pictographic signs - one of the few original writing systems
      of Yunnan. Nanzhao - the state of the Bai people that rivals the Tang for Yunnan
      - maintains trading posts near Lijiang.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231949
    m_Localized: At the point where the road from Merv crosses the spurs of the Kopet
      Dag and descends to the Nishapur plain stands a modest settlement of Sanabad
      with the tomb of Imam Ali al-Ridha - son of Caliph Husayn from the Alid line.
      Shia pilgrims have already begun making their way to the shrine, although the
      settlement does not yet bear the name Mashhad - the city of martyrdom. Abbasid
      officials look with suspicion upon the Alid devotees who prefer to pray over
      the imam's grave rather than in the caliph's mosque. Merchants at this waystation
      offer fresh camels, fodder, and lodgings; local potters make vessels from Nishapur's
      pink clay.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231950
    m_Localized: 'In a basin among the bare mountains of the Hejaz stands Mecca:
      the Kaaba - a cube of black stone draped in the kiswa - is the center around
      which pilgrims from across the Islamic world circuit seven times. The hajj
      each year brings together in one place merchants from Khorasan and Anatolia
      with traders from Zanzibar and Hindustan: while pilgrims pray their servants
      and partners trade livestock, cloth, spices, and price intelligence from three
      horizons away. The Abbasid governor of the Hejaz collects taxes in Mecca and
      keeps order during the hajj; revenue from the trade enriches local Qurayshi
      notables. Zamzam water drawn by pilgrims into flasks travels across the Muslim
      world as a sacred object.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231951
    m_Localized: 'Yathrib, renamed Madinat an-Nabi - City of the Prophet - during
      Muhammad''s lifetime, lies in an oasis amid the lava fields of the Hejaz. The
      Prophet''s Mosque with his tomb is the second holiest site of Islam: pilgrims
      who complete the hajj in Mecca walk here two days through the desert or hire
      a camel driver. Here the hadith and traditions are kept - the recorded words
      and deeds of the Prophet noted by his companions; Medina''s theologians are
      one of the chief schools of Muslim jurisprudential thought. The date palms
      of the oasis yield fruit distributed across the Caliphate as a blessed food;
      trade in livestock and wool sustains the citizens in the intervals between
      pilgrimage seasons.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231952
    m_Localized: An oasis in the Karakum Desert. An ancient fortress and military
      garrison control the trade routes.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231953
    m_Localized: 'On the western bank of the Tigris stands Mosul, and directly opposite
      it rise enormous tell mounds: buried beneath them is Nineveh, the capital of
      Assyrian kings who ruled from the Nile to the Zagros a thousand years ago.
      Mosul is the main crossing between Mesopotamia and the road to Syria: boatmen
      on kelekh hide-raft ferries keep service running even in flood season. The
      cotton cloth woven by Mosul craftsmen gave the word muslin to many western
      languages; tanneries supply harness and saddlebags for merchant caravans. The
      Abbasid governor collects here taxes from northern Jazira - the fertile triangle
      between the Tigris and Euphrates - and forwards them to Baghdad.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231954
    m_Localized: 'Nishapur grew into the principal city of Persian Khorasan after
      the Arab conquest: wide streets with covered bazaars, madrasas, and caravanserais
      speak of the wealth produced by a trading crossroads between Balkh and Rey.
      In the mountains to the northwest lie veins of turquoise - the stone Arabs
      call firuza; Nishapur''s sky-blue turquoise travels to Baghdad, Constantinople,
      and Chang''an set in gold and silver. Persian scholars under the patronage
      of Abbasid governors translate and annotate works on medicine and astrology.
      The local climate is mild: the peaches, melons, and grapes of Nishapur are
      already considered the finest in Khorasan in the eighth century.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231955
    m_Localized: Osh lies at the foot of Sulaiman-Too mountain in the southern part
      of the Fergana valley - the most fertile valley of Central Asia, surrounded
      on all sides by the ranges of the Pamir-Alai and Tian Shan. Fergana horses
      - the nergizt that the Chinese called "heavenly horses" and for which Emperor
      Wu launched military campaigns - are still the most valued commodity here.
      Cotton, pomegranates, and apricots from the valley travel westward while silk
      and bronze mirrors from Chang'an arrive through Samarkand. After the Arab army's
      victory over the Tang at the Talas river in 751 the valley passed under Abbasid
      authority; many Sogdian landowners converted to Islam while keeping their land
      and craft.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231956
    m_Localized: 'Pataliputra - now contracted to the modest town of Patna - stands
      where the Son joins the Ganges: once the capital of the Mauryas and the Guptas,
      and the broad earthen ramparts of that distant greatness are still visible
      at the riverbank. Now a provincial center of the Pala realm, monks from the
      Buddhist monastery of Nalanda a few days'' journey away come here for rice
      and cloth. River boats carrying Bengali rice, cane sugar, and sesame oil dock
      and depart with the current; on the market Tamilian merchants bargain with
      Bihari landowners. It was here that the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang, returning
      from India in 645, transferred manuscript sutras from river boats to elephants.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231957
    m_Localized: 'Rey stands at the southern foot of the Alborz mountains at the
      crossroads of roads to Khorasan and the Caspian shore: all routes from western
      Persia eastward converge here. The city is older than living memory - the Avestan
      Ragha is mentioned in the Zend-Avesta as one of the first creations of Ahura
      Mazda. Under the Abbasids Rey is the provincial capital of Jibal, a major market
      for grain and woolen cloth from the mountain pastures. The craftsmen of Rey
      produce excellent swords and daggers from Damascus blanks; the local bazaar
      sells copper and alum mined from the Alborz. Near the city a naphtha spring
      bubbles up, used to treat skin ailments and to grease the axles of merchant
      carts.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231958
    m_Localized: 'Samarkand - the Maracanda of the ancient Greeks - lies on the bank
      of the Zerafshan where the river spreads into irrigation canals feeding the
      melons and vineyards of all Sogdia. Sogdian merchant-sartirs maintain colonies
      in Chang''an, Dunhuang, and Persepolis: it is they who stitch the western Silk
      Road into a single chain. Samarkand paper - produced by a technique that Chinese
      prisoners transmitted to the Arabs after Talas - is gradually displacing papyrus
      and parchment across the Caliphate. On the bazaar one can buy Sogdian silver
      with hunting scenes, glass from Syria, and muslin from Mosul; payment is made
      simultaneously in Chinese kaiyuan tongbao coins and Arab dirhams. Arab governors
      sit in the citadel but commercial affairs are still managed by Sogdian dehqan
      landowners.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231959
    m_Localized: 'Suhar is the wealthiest city of the Omani coast: the domes of mosques
      and the towers of watch-forts are visible from the sea long before the harbor
      entrance. Voyages to India and China from Suhar are routine for local navigators
      who use teak dhows with stitched planks and steer by stars following oral sailing
      directions. An Arab geographer will later write that in Suhar live the richest
      merchants of the East. Copper from the Omani mines of Jebel Akhdar is the chief
      export: it travels in ingots to India and is exchanged for pepper and cotton
      cloth. The fishing fleet supplies dried fish to all of Hadramaut and part of
      the Persian coast.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231960
    m_Localized: A trade hub between Persia and Anatolia. Tabriz Bazaar is one of
      the largest covered markets in the world.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231961
    m_Localized: 'Tamralipti stands where the Ganges delta meets the Bay of Bengal:
      behind the distant mangrove shores lie shallows where crocodiles lurk but ships
      come nonetheless - so rich is the cargo of the Bengali port. The Chinese pilgrim
      Xuanzang described dozens of Buddhist monasteries here and twenty-four thousand
      monks in the mid-seventh century. From here ships depart for Ceylon, Sumatra,
      and further east; returning they bring spices, camphor, and Sumatran gold.
      Cotton and jute cloth from Bengali workshops travel to Ceylon and the Malabar
      ports. The Pala realm patronises the city: its rulers build monastery-viharas
      across eastern Hindustan.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231962
    m_Localized: A major Central Asian city near the Tien Shan mountains. Water from
      mountain rivers feeds gardens and fields.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231963
    m_Localized: "An ancient city of Gandhara \u2014 a crossroads of Greek and Indian
      cultures. University ruins hold the wisdom of ages."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231964
    m_Localized: 'Trebizond clings to the steep slope between the Pontic mountains
      and the Black Sea: wooded ridges loom above the city while Byzantine trading
      galleys and Armenian flatboats rock in the harbor below. This is the chief
      landing place for goods from the Caucasus - Iberian gold, Armenian horses,
      and Caucasian furs - which are reloaded here onto ships bound for Constantinople.
      The local theme of Chaldia guards the flank of the Roman Empire against Arab
      raids through the Armenian mountains. Greek merchants keep offices here and
      receive goods from Persia along the mountain road through Bayburt; Pontic wine
      and local hazelnuts travel westward with the prevailing wind.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231965
    m_Localized: 'Tarabulus al-Sham - Tripoli of Syria - lies on a narrow headland
      between the sea and the Lebanon mountains: three separate quarters divided
      by canals gave the city its name - three poleis. Under the Umayyads and the
      Abbasids this is the chief port of the Syrian coast between Tyre and Latakia.
      Arab and Egyptian vessels load here the cedar timber of Mount Lebanon - the
      most prized shipbuilding material on the Caliphate''s seas. Tripoli''s workshops
      produce glass and purple dye from local murex snails; the local market trades
      silk from the east and olive oil from the Lebanon mountains.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231966
    m_Localized: "An oasis in the Turfan Depression \u2014 the hottest place on the
      route. Grapes and melons grow amid the sands."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7217665176231967
    m_Localized: 'Yumen Guan - the Jade Gate - stands where the wall ends and the
      Gobi desert begins: a square tower of yellow brick ten paces high with a single
      door barred by a bolt of tamarisk wood. The name comes from Khotanese jade
      that once passed through here into China in thousand-catty bale-loads. Every
      merchant heading west or arriving from the west must present papers to the
      imperial guard here and pay the road toll. The poet Wang Zhihuan wrote of the
      gate lines memorized by every educated Tang person: beyond it not a single
      green leaf grows - only the yellow river wind. The garrison soldiers rotate
      every three years and dream of reassignment eastward.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7219978439745536
    m_Localized: Hami
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7219978485882880
    m_Localized: 'Hami is the first oasis beyond the Jade Gate, a garden amid gravel
      and salt flats: groundwater lifted by underground qanat channels feeds vegetable
      plots and vineyards. The local grapes are so sweet that they are dried in clay
      towers with wind-vents and sent to Chang''an as a delicacy for the imperial
      table. Turkic chieftains and Tang garrison officials share authority over the
      city by tacit agreement: the former provide horses and scouting, the latter
      pay in grain from the state granaries. All routes - the northern one along
      the Tian Shan and the southern one through the Lop Nor basin - diverge here,
      making Hami the first node of route choice for every caravan.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 9425860418330624
    m_Localized: Urumqi
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 9425860468662272
    m_Localized: 'In the wide valley at the northern foot of the Tian Shan where
      Turkic and Uyghur herds winter the Tang administration established a fortified
      post called Luntai which local inhabitants name differently. The site serves
      as a base for controlling the northern bypass route: when the main road through
      Hami is cut by steppe raids merchants turn north past Urumqi toward Karashahr
      and Kucha. The pasturelands yield excellent horses and wool; grain is brought
      here by camel from Hami. The Uyghur Khaganate established after 744 claims
      these steppes and the Tang commanders conduct here constant diplomatic negotiation
      between military sorties.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
