  - m_Id: 17330261711642645
    m_Localized: Local ruler has raised trade tariffs
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17330261711642646
    m_Localized: High Taxes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17330261711642647
    m_Localized: Military conflict has disrupted trade routes - prices and danger
      have surged
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17330261711642648
    m_Localized: War
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015099506688
    m_Localized: Do not ride to {0} now - troops hold the roads and looters give
      no one any rest.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644032
    m_Localized: I was in {0} last week. A whole quarter burned, the bazaar sits
      shut, half the merchants have fled.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644033
    m_Localized: They say soldiers are back in {0}. Prices are wild, but you can
      still find some goods there if you look.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644034
    m_Localized: Keep clear of the road to {0} - a gang has been raiding for three
      weeks, three caravans already stripped.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644035
    m_Localized: I was stopped myself near the gates of {0}. Five riders with bows.
      Lost two bales of silk and count myself lucky.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644036
    m_Localized: '{0} is restless these days - bandits own the approaches. But trade
      still moves if you travel in a large convoy.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644037
    m_Localized: Skirt around {0} - plague is there. Gate guards drive off anyone
      without a physician's seal.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644038
    m_Localized: I rode to {0} but did not enter. Smoke from bonfires hung above
      the walls and the smell - unmistakable. I turned back.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644039
    m_Localized: Sickness is making its rounds in {0}. Folk are thinning out, trade
      has all but stopped, but not every shop is shuttered - you can go, with great
      caution.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644040
    m_Localized: There is a festival in {0} right now - merchants poured in from
      all sides, prices fell, and spirits are high. Best time to go.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644041
    m_Localized: I just came from {0}. The square blazes with lights, drums beat
      till morning, and one dirham buys what two would any other day.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644042
    m_Localized: There is a feast in {0} - crowds everywhere, noise. Goods sell briskly,
      but the cut-purses are busy too.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644043
    m_Localized: A large convoy from the west arrived in {0} recently. The market
      is bursting with goods - no point going now, prices have dropped.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644044
    m_Localized: You are late to {0} with silk. They have been unloading a whole
      convoy since last week - you will not sell at a good price.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644045
    m_Localized: '{0} is awash in goods right now - a massive convoy just arrived.
      You can pick up something cheap, but there is nothing worth hauling there.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644046
    m_Localized: In {0} the tax-men strip you to the last coin - haul your goods
      elsewhere.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644047
    m_Localized: They say the ruler of {0} imposed a new tariff. Merchants are groaning,
      and weighmen stand at every entry point.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644048
    m_Localized: I was in {0} - taxes bite hard, I will not deny it. But their wares
      are good, if you do not buy too much.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644049
    m_Localized: Do not count on water in {0} - the wells are low, and prices for
      water and grain are the worst I have seen. Stock up here.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644050
    m_Localized: '{0} has had drought two years running. The river shallowed, the
      fields cracked, the people are sour. You can trade there, but do not expect
      much.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31115015145644051
    m_Localized: They say {0} has seen no rain since spring. Food is scarce in the
      bazaar, but everything else sells - people have no mind for trade, only for
      feeding themselves.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844525568000
    m_Localized: Healer
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705344
    m_Localized: Heal companions
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705345
    m_Localized: Archive
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705346
    m_Localized: Scrolls, cultures, artifacts
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705347
    m_Localized: Unknown
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705348
    m_Localized: Culture not defined
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705349
    m_Localized: The Tang Empire stretched from the Yellow Sea to the Tarim Basin;
      its capital Chang'an held over a million inhabitants in the seventh and eighth
      centuries and welcomed merchants from dozens of peoples. Speakers of Chinese
      with regional dialects use a shared logographic script that unifies the educated
      elite across vast distances. Confucianism governs the state order, Buddhism
      penetrates daily life ever deeper, and Daoism persists in temples and alchemical
      workshops. The empire's chief exports - silk, porcelain, paper, and tea - are
      indispensable to western markets. Imperial officials who passed the keju examination
      administer the realm and receive foreign embassies. In the ports of Guangzhou
      and in Chang'an itself, foreigners may live in their own quarters - Tang China
      is curious about the world, so long as the world pays its duties.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705350
    m_Localized: 'The steppe peoples - Turks, Uyghurs, and affiliated tribes - occupy
      a belt from Manchuria to Semirechye. Their languages belong to the Turkic family;
      runic inscriptions on Orkhon steles record epics and khagan proclamations.
      Before 763, most nomads follow Tengrism, faith in the sky and ancestral spirits;
      after the Uyghur khagan helped Tang suppress the An Lushan rebellion, he adopted
      Manichaeism and made it the state creed of the khaganate. The steppe''s chief
      wealth is horses, felt, furs, and livestock - horses above all, traded to Tang
      in exchange for bolts of silk. The nomads serve as indispensable intermediaries
      between China and western markets, letting bales of silk and spice-laden packs
      pass through their grazing lands. The line between trade and raid is thin here:
      today the khaganate sells horses, tomorrow it demands tribute.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705351
    m_Localized: Central Asia
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705352
    m_Localized: 'The Sogdians of Samarkand, Bukhara, and Penjikent were the Silk
      Road''s foremost merchants for centuries. They speak Sogdian - an Iranian tongue
      written in the Sogdian alphabet, which later gave rise to the Uyghur and Mongol
      scripts. Religiously they are diverse: Zoroastrian fire temples stand beside
      Manichaean prayer houses and Nestorian churches. In their colonies strung along
      the entire route - from Dunhuang to Chang''an - debt records and trade contracts
      in Sogdian serve as the commercial lingua franca of the road. Chief commodities
      include silk, lapis lazuli, musk, enslaved persons, and Sasanian-style silver
      vessels. By the end of the Tang period, Arab conquest and Islamization gradually
      absorb Sogdian identity, transforming Samarkand into a Muslim city.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705353
    m_Localized: In 651 the last Sasanian shah fell to Arab armies - but Persian
      civilization did not vanish. New Persian (Farsi) takes shape in this era from
      Middle Persian under Arabic influence; Zoroastrian communities persist in Yazd
      and Nishapur, paying the jizya and preserving their rites. Persian scholars
      and poets at the Abbasid court in Baghdad compose treatises on astronomy, medicine,
      and philosophy - the Arab-Persian synthesis fuels the caliphate's intellectual
      flowering. From the markets of Nishapur and Ray, turquoise, carpets, perfumes,
      and glassware move in both eastern and western directions. Persia is a land
      conquered by the sword but not defeated in culture; its merchants and diplomats
      carry Persian etiquette to courts from India to China.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705354
    m_Localized: 'In 762 the caliph al-Mansur laid the foundations of Baghdad on
      the banks of the Tigris - and within decades it grew into the largest city
      in the world. The Abbasid caliphate speaks Arabic; Arabic script became the
      language of the Quran, law, scholarship, and trade contracts from the Maghreb
      to Khurasan. Islam defines the legal order and the calendar: Friday prayers
      in the mosques mark the rhythm of the markets. The chief wealth of Mesopotamia
      is rice from the irrigated fields of lower Iraq, dates, linen, and the products
      of Baghdad''s workshops. The House of Wisdom at the caliph''s court gathers
      scholar-translators of Greek, Persian, and Indian texts, making Baghdad the
      intellectual center of the known world. For a merchant, a working knowledge
      of Arabic and Islamic etiquette opens the doors to the wealthiest markets of
      the age.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705355
    m_Localized: The Levant - the coastline of Syria and Palestine - passed from
      Byzantium to the Umayyad Caliphate in 636-638, then submitted to the Abbasids
      after 750; its chief city Damascus was the capital of the entire Islamic world
      until that same year. Arabic became the language of power, but Syriac Christians,
      Jews, and remnants of the Greek population continue to live in their own communities.
      Damascene silk and blades of Damascus steel travel across the entire Old World;
      the glassworks of Sidon and Tyre supply goods to the caliphate's markets. Levantine
      merchants are seasoned middlemen of Mediterranean trade, linking Europe, Egypt,
      and Mesopotamia. In the bazaars of Damascus and Aleppo, western spices meet
      Chinese silk, and Arabic serves as the language of transaction for all.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705356
    m_Localized: 'Anatolia in the Tang era is Byzantine territory divided into themes:
      military-administrative districts holding the frontier against Arab raids.
      Greek dominates in cities and churches, though Armenian and Syriac are still
      heard in the countryside. Orthodox Christianity is the Empire''s state faith;
      the iconoclast controversies of the eighth and ninth centuries tear society
      apart, dividing monasteries and the capital court. Asia Minor''s ports - Ephesus,
      Trebizond - receive ships from Constantinople and trade caravans from the east.
      Copper and iron from Pontic mines, woollen cloth, and Cappadocian horses are
      sold here. A traveller from Iran or Syria feels the world change when crossing
      the mountain passes: different smells, different prayers, different coins.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705357
    m_Localized: The Eastern Roman Empire - Byzantium - has ruled from Constantinople
      for three centuries, regarding itself as the lawful continuation of Rome; its
      state language is Greek, with official Latin preserved in titles and law. Orthodox
      Christianity permeates everything from palace etiquette to street commerce.
      Constantinople stands at the Bosphorus crossing, where routes from the Black
      Sea, Europe, and Asia Minor converge; the imperial monopoly on purple silk
      brocade makes it the foremost article of diplomatic gifts. The naval secret
      of Greek fire keeps Constantinople undefeated by sea. Imperial diplomatic missions
      reach Khazaria, Georgia, and even China, weaving alliances against Arab expansion.
      For a merchant, access to the Constantinopolitan markets is a privilege negotiated
      through embassies, not purchased at a stall.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705358
    m_Localized: 'India in the Tang era is not a single kingdom: the north is divided
      between the Pratiharas and the Palas, while the south belongs to the Cholas
      and Rashtrakutas. Sanskrit is the sacred language of scholars, monks, and Brahmins
      across the subcontinent; local Prakrits and Dravidian tongues are the languages
      of markets and villages. Buddhism flourishes under the Palas in the east -
      the monastery of Nalanda receives students from Tibet, China, and Central Asia;
      Hinduism with its temple economies is stronger in the west and south. Chief
      export goods are cotton and cotton cloth, pepper and spices, gemstones from
      the Deccan and Ceylon, and indigo. The seaports of Cambay in the west and Tamralipti
      in the east link India to Arabia and Southeast Asia. For a merchant in Indian
      cities it matters greatly to know whom you are dealing with - caste and religion
      determine the rules of commercial exchange.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705359
    m_Localized: 'The Arabian Peninsula is the cradle of Islam: from Mecca and Medina
      in the seventh century armies marched out that within a century had conquered
      lands from Spain to the Indus. Arabic - the language of the Quran - became
      the chief commercial tongue of the caliphate; its speakers settled across the
      conquered world, bringing their faith with them. Pre-Islamic Arabia lived on
      aromatics - frankincense and myrrh from Hadramawt - and sea trade through the
      Persian Gulf and the Red Sea; these routes thrive under the caliphate as well.
      Merchant communities of Oman and the Hijaz outfit ships bound for India, Ceylon,
      and the ports of East Africa. The camel is the peninsula''s primary means of
      transport: without it neither the desert routes nor the seasonal trade in myrrh
      and spices would be possible. For a merchant, knowledge of Arab commercial
      customs and respect for the Friday prayer is the key to business ties throughout
      the caliphate.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33352844571705360
    m_Localized: 'The Tibetan Empire under kings of the Yarlung dynasty was a formidable
      power from the seventh to the ninth century: it wrested Tarim oases from Tang,
      entered alliances with the Arabs against China, and controlled the mountain
      passes through which pilgrims and goods traveled. The Tibetan language is written
      in a script created in the seventh century under King Songtsen Gampo, modeled
      on Indian Brahmi for translating Buddhist sutras. Buddhism entered Tibet from
      India and China, merging with the native Bon religion; monasteries became both
      sacred and economic centers. The chief goods Tibet brings to market are yak
      wool, musk, alluvial gold, and highland medicinal herbs. After the collapse
      of the Tibetan Empire in 842 power fragmented, but monastic culture survived.
      For a merchant trading in the Tarim or Nepal, the Tibetan language is a necessary
      tool, and knowledge of Buddhist customs opens the gates of monasteries.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
